<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<robot name="VICA" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

<!-- Keep includes relative so this file expands before the package is installed. -->
<xacro:include filename="materials.xacro" />

<!-- Optional manipulator, off by default.
     ============================================================
     Two robots are built from this one file: the driving robot,
     which stays in step with the physical platform and carries
     every width, cornering and HIL result; and an arm-carrying
     variant used to study what a manipulator would cost.

     They must come from one source. The export chain already
     showed what happens otherwise: urdf/vica.urdf sat holding a
     0.293 m wheel separation for days after this file was
     corrected to 0.364, and nothing in the chain said so. That was
     one artefact drifting from one source. Two hand-maintained
     robot descriptions would be the same failure with nowhere to
     look for the truth.

     use_arm:=false is the default deliberately: expanding this
     file with no arguments must produce exactly the robot the
     driving results were measured on. Anything that changes the
     footprint, the mass, the inertia or the lidar plane belongs
     inside the guarded block, never outside it.

         export_isaac_urdf.sh          -> urdf/vica.urdf
         export_isaac_urdf.sh (arm)    -> urdf/vica_arm.urdf

     The arm is a Kinova Gen3 lite on a riser. [미검증] What is
     modelled here is its mass and its stowed envelope, not its
     kinematics: the six joints need kortex_description, which is
     not in this workspace and whose installation is an approval
     item. So use_arm:=true today gives a robot that weighs and
     balances like the real thing and cannot move an elbow.

     That split is deliberate rather than a shortcut. Everything the
     driving stack cares about is the stowed case: the footprint it
     plans against, the plane the lidar sweeps, and the load the
     brakes have to stop. All three are settled by a rigid lump of
     the right mass in the right place. Reaching for a button is the
     part that needs joints, and that part needs the package.
     -->
<xacro:arg name="use_arm" default="false" />
<xacro:property name="use_arm" value="$(arg use_arm)" />
<!-- Riser height. An argument because it is the one number worth sweeping:
     it buys reach at the cost of CoM height, and both sides of that trade
     are printed by scripts/mass_properties.py.

     0.10, down from 0.25. The 0.25 was set to clear a 1.10 m button that a
     deck-mounted arm was calculated to miss by 7 mm, using the stand-in
     cylinder before kortex_description existed. Sampled against the real
     kinematics from the measured deck, the trade reads:

         riser   forward reach at 1.10 m   past the robot's nose   CoM
         0.00           0.311 m                   +6 mm          0.310 m
         0.10           0.508 m                 +203 mm          0.336 m
         0.15           0.518 m                 +213 mm          0.347 m
         0.25           0.468 m                 +163 mm          0.369 m

     At 0 the gripper barely clears the robot's own front edge, so the robot
     would have to touch the wall to press anything. 0.10 buys almost all the
     standoff 0.15 does and carries the mass 11 mm lower. -->
<xacro:arg name="arm_riser_h" default="0.10" />
<xacro:property name="arm_riser_h" value="$(arg arm_riser_h)" />

<!-- Geometry exported from CAD is expressed in metres through a 0.001 mesh scale.
     Coordinates below match vica_ros2_ws@dev (commit 621e181), which is the
     canonical source: tape-measured on the robot and cross-checked against the
     STL bounding boxes. Keep them as expressions, not constants; every bug
     found in this model came from a hand-copied number drifting out of step. -->
<xacro:property name="body_center_z" value="0.041" />
<xacro:property name="base_link_height" value="0.19" />
<!-- Simplified chassis collisions derived from the lower body and mast bounds.
     Held as components so the deck top can be derived rather than copied: it
     is 0.259 above base_link, and a second place recording that number is a
     second place for it to go stale. -->
<xacro:property name="chassis_body_size_x" value="0.61" />
<xacro:property name="chassis_body_size_y" value="0.28" />
<xacro:property name="chassis_body_size_z" value="0.401" />
<xacro:property name="chassis_body_origin_z" value="0.0585" />
<xacro:property name="chassis_body_size"
                value="${chassis_body_size_x} ${chassis_body_size_y} ${chassis_body_size_z}" />
<xacro:property name="chassis_body_origin" value="0 0 ${chassis_body_origin_z}" />
<!-- Deck top, base_link relative. Absolute height is this plus base_link_height.
     Derived from the collision box, and the collision box is 89 mm taller than
     the body it stands for. Nothing mounts on it. -->
<xacro:property name="chassis_deck_z"
                value="${chassis_body_origin_z + chassis_body_size_z / 2}" />
<!-- The deck a bolt can actually go into, measured off meshes/base_link.stl:
     the upward facing triangles there total 0.2608 m2 over 0.590 x 0.453 m,
     which is the top plate. chassis_deck_z above is 0.259 and the mesh has
     no upward facing surface within 20 mm of it at all: the arm riser was
     mounted there and stood 89 mm in the air beside the mast, which is what
     the first render showed. Re-measure with a mesh scan if base_link.stl
     is ever regenerated from CAD. -->
<xacro:property name="chassis_deck_measured_z" value="0.170" />
<!-- The handle post, as the redesign leaves it. Bounds read off
     meshes/base_link_mast.stl rather than copied from a drawing:
       x  -0.478 .. -0.238   (0.24 deep, centre -0.358)
       z   0.259 ..  0.885   base_link relative, absolute 0.449 .. 1.075
     The old numbers described base_link.stl, whose post stopped at 0.863
     absolute and ran back to -0.505. A collider still standing there would
     have put a wall behind the robot that the render does not show. -->
<xacro:property name="chassis_mast_size" value="0.24 0.04 0.626" />
<xacro:property name="chassis_mast_origin" value="-0.358 0 0.572" />

<!-- Sensor origins are relative to base_link. -->
<xacro:property name="laser_x" value="0.185" />
<xacro:property name="laser_y" value="0.0" />
<xacro:property name="laser_z" value="0.192" />
<!-- Camera on the handle post. Tracks vica_ros2_ws@dev, which is the source
     of truth for robot geometry. This file follows it, never the reverse.

       camera_x  -0.225   the 26 mm camera box's back face lands on -0.238,
                          the post's front face above. Flush, not guessed.
       camera_z   0.855   absolute 1.045 m

     2026-09-02: matched to the real robot, replacing the 1.000 m that was
     chosen here on 2026-08-24 when nothing had been tape-measured yet. The
     robot has been measured three times since and the last one stands:

         2026-08-24   ground 0.910   camera_z 0.720   first tape measure
         2026-08-29   ground 1.025   camera_z 0.835
         2026-08-30   ground 1.045   camera_z 0.855   after a remount

     Re-measure on the robot before trusting this; it moves whenever the
     camera is detached. The real config's comment carries the full history.

     ** Every stage USD and every result recorded before 2026-09-02 was made
     with the camera at 1.000 m and dead level. They are not comparable to
     anything measured after. -->
<xacro:property name="camera_x" value="-0.225" />
<xacro:property name="camera_y" value="0.0" />
<xacro:property name="camera_z" value="0.855" />
<!-- The mast sits 0.7 degrees nose-up, measured on the real robot by fitting
     the floor plane in the depth cloud (RANSAC, 3 runs, -2.35/-2.31/-2.24
     degrees residual against a URDF that then said -3.0). Sign convention:
     URDF pitch is positive down, so nose-up is negative.

     It matters here for the same reason it matters on the robot. Isaac's
     depth camera hangs off camera_link, so a tilt the TF does not know about
     puts every returned point at the wrong height: at 2 m, 0.7 degrees is
     2.4 cm, and the floor lifts into the costmap's slice as an obstacle.
     Modelling the tilt costs nothing and removes a whole class of "the
     corridor filled in" confusion from the comparison runs. -->
<xacro:property name="camera_pitch" value="-0.0124" />
<!-- Two DYP-A22 ultrasonic rangers on the front bumper, fitted to the robot
     2026-08-31. Mounted horizontally; the housing cannot be tilted.
     vica_ros2_ws@dev and docs/handoff_jetson_ultrasonic_i2c.md are the source.

       x  0.305    the bumper face itself
       y  +-0.107  either side of centre
       z -0.0987   ground 0.0913, minus base_link_height 0.190

     Frame names have to match the driver's ultrasonic_frame_ids, so they are
     copied rather than invented.

     Why they exist: the depth camera at 1.045 m looks over anything low and
     close. These sit at ankle height where the lidar plane (0.382) and the
     camera both have nothing to say. [미검증] in sim. The frames are here so
     a range sensor can be attached to them, but no Isaac sensor is publishing
     into them yet and no costmap layer consumes them. The comparison runs
     below do not use ultrasound. -->
<xacro:property name="usonic_x" value="0.305" />
<xacro:property name="usonic_y" value="0.107" />
<xacro:property name="usonic_z" value="-0.0987" />

<!-- Wheel placement. wheel_separation is the measured centre-to-centre distance.
     It is NOT the odometry constant. The real robot's encoder_feedback uses
     wheel_base_m = 0.37, an effective tread fitted by rotation test that absorbs
     tyre scrub. The two are different quantities and must not be unified. -->
<xacro:property name="wheel_separation" value="0.364" />
<xacro:property name="wheel_y" value="${wheel_separation / 2}" />
<xacro:property name="caster_steer_y" value="0.0845" />
<xacro:property name="caster_y" value="0.0795" />
<xacro:property name="caster_wheel_dy" value="${round(caster_y - caster_steer_y, 6)}" />

<!-- Conservative import defaults; tune these against the physical platform. -->
<xacro:property name="wheel_effort" value="100.0" />
<xacro:property name="wheel_velocity" value="20.0" />
<xacro:property name="wheel_damping" value="0.1" />
<!-- Primitive drive-wheel collisions derived from the STL bounds. -->
<xacro:property name="drive_wheel_radius" value="0.065" />
<xacro:property name="drive_wheel_width" value="0.0678" />
<!-- Primitive caster-wheel collisions derived from the STL bounds. -->
<xacro:property name="caster_wheel_radius" value="0.0375" />
<xacro:property name="caster_wheel_width" value="0.04" />
<!-- CAD places the caster 4.5 mm clear of the ground, so a collider at the true
     radius leaves PhysX resting the robot on its drive wheels and pitching it
     ~0.635 deg back, tilting the lidar plane with it. Oversizing the collider by
     that gap stands the robot level; the 4.5 mm stands in for tyre compression. -->
<xacro:property name="caster_collision_radius" value="0.042" />

<link name="base_footprint"/>

<joint name="base_footprint_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="0 0 ${base_link_height}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="base_footprint"/>
  <child link="base_link"/>
</joint>

<link name="base_link">
  <!-- Chassis mass, weighed 2026-08-07.
       ========================================================
       The robot is 20 kg on a scale. This link carried 90.847,
       and the eight links together came to 94.280, which is 4.71 times
       the real machine. The exported inertia came out of CAD, so
       the likeliest cause is a material density that was never
       set; the shape is right and the density is not.

       Only this link is corrected. The other seven are left
       alone because their values are all plausible for the parts
       they describe: 0.566 kg for a 65 mm rubber drive wheel,
       0.115 kg for a caster wheel, 0.164 kg for the lidar
       (an RPLIDAR is about 0.19). Scaling those down with the
       chassis would have made them physically absurd to make an
       arithmetic total come out right.

       20 - 3.4325 (the other seven) = 16.5675, and the inertia
       scales with it at 0.182367 for unchanged geometry.

       What this changes, beyond the number:
         rotational inertia izz  4.167 -> 0.760 kg m^2
         drive wheel normal load  288 N -> 61 N
         caster scrub resistance 17.4 N -> 3.7 N

       And the centre of mass moves, because the wheels and
       casters (low and spread out) now carry a fifth of the
       robot instead of a twenty-fifth:
         x  +0.00087 -> -0.01089   (further behind the axle)
         z   0.22026 ->  0.20241   (lower)
       which takes the forward tipping deceleration from 6.82 to
       7.99 m/s^2. Correcting the mass made the robot harder to
       tip, not easier.

       [미검증] The distribution within this link is still CAD's.
       Weighing the drive and caster axles separately would give
       the real x, and a tilt test the real z; until then the two
       numbers above are the shape's, not the robot's. -->
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0.004031215194176557 3.002726997808124e-17 0.03506562657816282" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="16.5675"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.438065" iyy="0.660628" izz="0.759891" ixy="0.0" iyz="-0.0" ixz="0.062604"/>
  </inertial>
  <!-- base_link_mast.stl, not base_link.stl: the same body with the rear
       full-width rail and skirt cut away and the handle post carried up to
       1.075 absolute, which is where the camera now mounts. Copied from
       vica_ros2_ws@dev (55e1b0f). The collision below is still derived from
       the primitives above, not from this mesh. -->
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0 0 -${body_center_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/base_link_mast.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="body_blue"/>
  </visual>
  <!-- Keep the lower collider narrow enough to leave the wheel contact region clear. -->
  <collision name="chassis_body_collision">
    <origin xyz="${chassis_body_origin}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="${chassis_body_size}"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
  <collision name="chassis_mast_collision">
    <origin xyz="${chassis_mast_origin}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="${chassis_mast_size}"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="left_wheel_1">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="2.0539125955565396e-15 0.03668765550201605 0.0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.5663250710944822"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.000634" iyy="0.001025" izz="0.000634" ixy="0.0" iyz="-0.0" ixz="0.0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="-0.154 ${-wheel_y} 0.084" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/left_wheel_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <!-- URDF cylinders are Z-aligned; rotate onto this wheel's Y axis. -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${pi / 2} 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <cylinder radius="${drive_wheel_radius}" length="${drive_wheel_width}"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="right_wheel_1">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="-0.00046568184733652984 -0.03568765550201608 0.0009570426748952837" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.5663250710944814"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.000634" iyy="0.001025" izz="0.000634" ixy="0.0" iyz="0.0" ixz="-0.0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="-0.154 ${wheel_y} 0.084" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/right_wheel_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <!-- URDF cylinders are Z-aligned; rotate onto this wheel's Y axis. -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${pi / 2} 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <cylinder radius="${drive_wheel_radius}" length="${drive_wheel_width}"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="front_left_caster_wheel_1">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="-1.6653345369377348e-15 0.01574568542726719 0.0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.11451893837110633"/>
    <inertia ixx="4.7e-05" iyy="7.8e-05" izz="4.7e-05" ixy="-0.0" iyz="0.0" ixz="0.0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0.252 ${-caster_y} 0.107" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/front_left_caster_wheel_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <!-- URDF cylinders are Z-aligned; rotate onto this wheel's Y axis. -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${pi / 2} 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <cylinder radius="${caster_collision_radius}" length="${caster_wheel_width}"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="front_right_caster_wheel_1">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="5.162537064506978e-15 -0.015745685427267203 0.0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.11451893837110629"/>
    <inertia ixx="4.7e-05" iyy="7.8e-05" izz="4.7e-05" ixy="-0.0" iyz="0.0" ixz="-0.0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0.252 ${caster_y} 0.107" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/front_right_caster_wheel_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <!-- URDF cylinders are Z-aligned; rotate onto this wheel's Y axis. -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${pi / 2} 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <cylinder radius="${caster_collision_radius}" length="${caster_wheel_width}"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="front_right_caster_steer_1">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="-0.004080894044594496 0.0037449849629774684 -0.017666622102230287" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.9536308497491053"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.000637" iyy="0.000626" izz="0.000908" ixy="4e-05" iyz="8.5e-05" ixz="-8.2e-05"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0.222 ${caster_steer_y} 0.0415" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/front_right_caster_steer_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <!-- CAD bounding box of the steer bracket; the STL became a convex hull on import. -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 0.003" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="0.08 0.08 0.0755"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="front_left_caster_steer_1">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="-0.004080894044594496 -0.003744984962977496 -0.017666622102230287" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.9536308497491053"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.000637" iyy="0.000626" izz="0.000908" ixy="-4e-05" iyz="-8.5e-05" ixz="-8.2e-05"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0.222 ${-caster_steer_y} 0.0415" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/front_left_caster_steer_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <!-- CAD bounding box of the steer bracket; the STL became a convex hull on import. -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 0.003" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="0.08 0.08 0.0755"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<link name="laser_frame">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.010868727234877334" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.16353480730457975"/>
    <inertia ixx="5.9e-05" iyy="5.9e-05" izz="0.000105" ixy="0.0" iyz="-0.0" ixz="-0.0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="-0.185 -0.0 ${-(laser_z + body_center_z)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <mesh filename="package://vica_description/meshes/lidar_1.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="silver"/>
  </visual>
  <!-- No collision: a lidar has nothing to collide with, and the STL becomes a
       convex hull on Isaac import that the scan then sees as an obstacle. -->
</link>

<link name="camera_link">
  <!-- RViz placeholder; Isaac Sim uses its own D455 USD sensor asset. -->
  <!-- 113 g, the D455's datasheet mass.
       Not optional, for the same reason the ultrasonic links say so: with
       merge_fixed_joints off, a link carrying geometry becomes its own rigid
       body, and a rigid body with no inertial gets no mass. PhysX then reports
       "possibly invalid inertia tensor of {1.0, 1.0, 1.0} and a negative mass"
       on every play, and the articulation's mass matrix goes with it. The
       symptom is position dependent and looks like anything but a mass bug:
       at x=-22 the wheels would not turn and the body slid 0.38 m and stopped,
       at x=0 the wheels spun 5050 degrees while the body never moved and sat
       48 mm low with its chassis on the floor. An earlier comment here claimed
       camera_link could get away with having no inertial. It could not. -->
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.113"/>
    <inertia ixx="1.53e-4" iyy="1.43e-5" izz="1.51e-4"
             ixy="0.0" iyz="0.0" ixz="0.0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <!-- D455 nominal depth x width x height: 26 x 124 x 29 mm. -->
      <box size="0.026 0.124 0.029"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="camera_black"/>
  </visual>
</link>
<link name="camera_optical_frame"/>
<!-- The RealSense driver publishes camera_color_optical_frame and
     camera_depth_optical_frame at runtime, so the physical robot's URDF must
     not declare them: two publishers for one frame is a TF fight.

     Isaac runs no driver, so here the URDF is the only thing that can supply
     them, and it has to: the image headers coming out of the Isaac graphs
     carry those frame names, and nvblox subscribes by them.

     Simplification: both sit at camera_link, ignoring the few-centimetre
     baseline between the D455's colour and depth imagers that the real driver
     reports. Harmless for costmaps at this range; check it before trusting
     close-up colour-to-depth registration. -->
<link name="camera_color_optical_frame"/>
<link name="camera_depth_optical_frame"/>

<joint name="left_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="0.154 ${wheel_y} -0.125" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="base_link"/>
  <child link="left_wheel_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="${wheel_damping}" friction="0.0"/>
  <limit effort="${wheel_effort}" velocity="${wheel_velocity}"/>
</joint>

<joint name="right_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="0.154 ${-wheel_y} -0.125" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="base_link"/>
  <child link="right_wheel_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="${wheel_damping}" friction="0.0"/>
  <limit effort="${wheel_effort}" velocity="${wheel_velocity}"/>
</joint>

<joint name="front_left_caster_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="-0.03 ${caster_wheel_dy} -0.0655" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="front_left_caster_steer_1"/>
  <child link="front_left_caster_wheel_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 -1.0 0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="front_right_caster_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="-0.03 ${-caster_wheel_dy} -0.0655" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="front_right_caster_steer_1"/>
  <child link="front_right_caster_wheel_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 -1.0 0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="front_right_caster_steer_joint" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="-0.222 ${-caster_steer_y} -0.0825" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="base_link"/>
  <child link="front_right_caster_steer_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 0.0 -1.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="front_left_caster_steer_joint" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="-0.222 ${caster_steer_y} -0.0825" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="base_link"/>
  <child link="front_left_caster_steer_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 0.0 -1.0"/>
</joint>

<!-- Sensor positions are expressed relative to the body-centered base_link. -->
<joint name="laser_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="${laser_x} ${laser_y} ${laser_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="base_link"/>
  <child link="laser_frame"/>
</joint>

<!-- The probe bodies, drawn so the mounting point is visible in a render
     instead of being an invisible frame nobody can check.

     DYP-A22 is a sealed cylindrical probe, 20 mm across and about 15 mm of
     barrel proud of its mount. Modelled as a cylinder on the x axis, which is
     the direction it looks; a URDF cylinder stands on z, so it is laid down
     by a 90 degree pitch.

     Placed to end flush with x = 0.305, not to stick out past it. That is the
     bumper face and the front edge of the footprint, and vica_ros2_ws@dev's
     comment is explicit that a probe protruding beyond it means usonic_x AND
     the footprint front must both grow. Neither has grown on the robot, so
     flush is what the robot's own numbers describe. If a tape measure says
     otherwise, this mesh is the thing that was wrong, not the footprint.

     Visual only. A 20 mm bump on the bumper face changes nothing the planner
     reasons about, and giving it a collider would put a second obstacle
     inside the footprint the robot already plans against. -->
<xacro:property name="usonic_radius" value="0.010" />
<xacro:property name="usonic_len" value="0.015" />

<xacro:macro name="usonic_probe" params="name y">
  <link name="${name}">
    <!-- 30 g, the probe and its lead.
         Not optional. The importer runs with merge_fixed_joints off, so a link
         carrying geometry becomes its own rigid body rather than folding into
         base_link, and a rigid body with no inertial gets no mass at all.
         Two of those on fixed joints took the articulation's mass matrix with
         them: the wheels turned, the body did not move, and it sank 21 mm into
         the floor. Body count went 9 to 11 and the robot stopped driving.
         camera_link had the same defect and the same excuse; see its link. -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="${-usonic_len/2} 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="0.03"/>
      <inertia ixx="1.5e-6" iyy="1.5e-6" izz="1.5e-6" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="${-usonic_len/2} 0 0" rpy="0 ${pi/2} 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <cylinder radius="${usonic_radius}" length="${usonic_len}"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="camera_black"/>
    </visual>
  </link>
  <joint name="${name}_joint" type="fixed">
    <origin xyz="${usonic_x} ${y} ${usonic_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="${name}"/>
  </joint>
</xacro:macro>

<xacro:usonic_probe name="usonic_front_left"  y="${usonic_y}" />
<xacro:usonic_probe name="usonic_front_right" y="${-usonic_y}" />

<joint name="camera_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="${camera_x} ${camera_y} ${camera_z}" rpy="0 ${camera_pitch} 0"/>
  <parent link="base_link"/>
  <child link="camera_link"/>
</joint>

<!-- REP-103 optical convention: +Z forward, +X right, +Y down. -->
<joint name="camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="-${pi / 2} 0 -${pi / 2}"/>
  <parent link="camera_link"/>
  <child link="camera_optical_frame"/>
</joint>

<!-- Same rotation, under the names the RealSense driver would publish. See the
     note at the link declarations for why these exist only in simulation. -->
<joint name="camera_color_optical_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="-${pi / 2} 0 -${pi / 2}"/>
  <parent link="camera_link"/>
  <child link="camera_color_optical_frame"/>
</joint>

<joint name="camera_depth_optical_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="-${pi / 2} 0 -${pi / 2}"/>
  <parent link="camera_link"/>
  <child link="camera_depth_optical_frame"/>
</joint>

<!-- Manipulator mount. Nothing below this line exists unless use_arm:=true. -->
<xacro:if value="${use_arm}">

  <!-- Where an arm may be bolted, and only there.
       ==========================================================
       x = 0, the deck centre, is not a placeholder. The drive axle
       contact line is at base_link x = +0.154 and the loaded CoM sits
       0.153 m behind it, so the forward tipping margin is 0.153 m and
       nothing else. Stacking mass vertically over x = 0 barely touches
       it; 20 kg on this deck takes the tipping deceleration from
       6.82 to 5.78 m/s^2, against a 2.5 m/s^2 braking limit. Reaching
       forward is what spends it: the same 20 kg held out at x = 0.55
       drops it to 1.57, which is below the limit the controller
       already plans against.

       So reach is bought with a riser, not by moving this joint
       forward. A 0.3 m riser cuts the reach needed for a 1.2 m
       elevator button from 1.16 m to 0.98 m and costs almost nothing
       in stability.

       z = 0.259 is the chassis collision top (origin 0.0585 + half of
       0.401), which is 0.449 m above the ground. The mast occupies
       x -0.505 to -0.265, so the usable deck is x in [-0.265, +0.305].

       Two things an arm mounted here must not do, both of which are
       cheaper to design around than to discover:
         - block the lidar plane at 0.382 m above ground. The robot
           would read its own arm as a wall.
         - stow outside the nav2 footprint hexagon, whose forward edge
           is x = +0.305. Widening that polygon invalidates every
           width result measured against it.
       -->
  <!-- Riser. Why there is one at all:

       A Gen3 lite reaches 0.76 m. Bolted straight to the deck at
       0.449 m above ground it cannot touch a 1.10 m elevator button.
       Park the robot with its front edge 0.10 m off the wall and the
       button sits 0.405 m ahead and 0.651 m up, which is 0.767 m
       away. Seven millimetres short, and it gets worse fast: back
       off to 0.5 m and the button is 0.82 m away.

       0.25 m of riser drops that to 0.57 m. It costs CoM height and
       nothing else, because the mass goes straight up over x = 0
       where the tipping arm barely notices it. mass_properties.py
       prints both variants; the forward tipping limit goes 7.99 to
       4.39 m/s^2, still 1.76x the 2.5 m/s^2 the smoother can
       command.

       The riser starts at the deck top, 0.449 m above ground, which
       is above the 0.382 m lidar plane. It cannot occlude the scan.
       Its 0.12 m square section sits well inside the footprint
       hexagon, so the polygon every width result was measured
       against does not move. -->
  <xacro:property name="arm_riser_side" value="0.12" />
  <xacro:property name="arm_riser_mass" value="2.0" />

  <!-- The riser is optional, and 0 is a real choice rather than a degenerate
       one: at 0 the link and its 2 kg do not exist and the arm bolts straight
       to the deck.

       It was 0.25 by default because a 0.76 m arm on the deck was calculated
       to miss a 1.10 m button by 7 mm. That calculation was done against the
       stand-in cylinder, before kortex_description was installed. Sampled
       against the real kinematics, the arm reaches 1.10 m from the deck with
       no riser at all, in 574 of 16807 sampled poses, extending 0.492 m
       forward. The riser buys margin, not reach, and it costs 2 kg at the top
       of the robot. -->
  <xacro:if value="${arm_riser_h > 0}">
    <link name="arm_riser_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${arm_riser_h / 2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="${arm_riser_side} ${arm_riser_side} ${arm_riser_h}"/>
        </geometry>
        <material name="silver"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${arm_riser_h / 2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="${arm_riser_side} ${arm_riser_side} ${arm_riser_h}"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${arm_riser_h / 2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="${arm_riser_mass}"/>
        <inertia
          ixx="${arm_riser_mass * (arm_riser_side ** 2 + arm_riser_h ** 2) / 12}"
          iyy="${arm_riser_mass * (arm_riser_side ** 2 + arm_riser_h ** 2) / 12}"
          izz="${arm_riser_mass * 2 * arm_riser_side ** 2 / 12}"
          ixy="0.0" iyz="0.0" ixz="0.0"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="arm_riser_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${chassis_deck_measured_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child link="arm_riser_link"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="arm_mount_link"/>

    <joint name="arm_mount_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${arm_riser_h}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <parent link="arm_riser_link"/>
      <child link="arm_mount_link"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:if>

  <xacro:unless value="${arm_riser_h > 0}">
    <link name="arm_mount_link"/>

    <joint name="arm_mount_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${chassis_deck_measured_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child link="arm_mount_link"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:unless>

  <!-- The real arm, from ros-jazzy-kortex-description 0.2.6.
       The stand in cylinder that used to sit here is gone: it existed only
       because the link lengths and joint limits were unknown, and they are
       not any more. What it gave was a mass and a stowed envelope; what this
       gives in addition is six joints, real meshes, and the limits that say
       where the gripper can and cannot reach.

       The include lives inside this xacro:if on purpose. VICA.xacro has to
       expand on a machine that has never heard of kortex_description, which
       is every machine that only drives, including the physical robot's.
       At the top of the file the include would run unconditionally and
       use_arm:=false would stop working there.

       prefix is not decoration. The macro names its own root
       "${prefix}base_link", and this robot already has a base_link; without
       a prefix the two collide and the arm silently reparents the chassis.

       robot_ip and the gripper name are required parameters of a macro
       written for a real arm on a network. Nothing in simulation reads them.
       0.0.0.0 is there to be obviously not an address. -->
  <xacro:include filename="$(find kortex_description)/arms/gen3_lite/6dof/urdf/gen3_lite_macro.xacro" />

  <xacro:load_arm
      parent="arm_mount_link"
      prefix="gen3_"
      dof="6"
      vision="false"
      robot_ip="0.0.0.0"
      gripper_joint_name="gen3_gripper"
      use_fake_hardware="true"
      sim_isaac="false"
      sim_gazebo="false">
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </xacro:load_arm>

</xacro:if>

</robot>
